<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19870068</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2132093</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE DETECTION OF POLIOMYELITIS VIRUS IN SO CALLED ABORTIVE TYPES OF THE DISEASE</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Paul</surname><given-names>John R.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Trask</surname><given-names>J. D.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x1ee1660N0x36ce5f8">From the Departments of Medicine and Pediatrics, Yale University, New Haven</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>31</day><month>8</month><year>1932</year></pub-date><volume>56</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>319</fpage><lpage>343</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>29</day><month>4</month><year>1932</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1932, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Experiments are reported which describe the isolation of poliomyelitis virus from the throats of two patients during an attack of so called abortive poliomyelitis (Wickman type), or what we have termed characteristic minor illnesses in association with poliomyelitis. This finding represents added evidence in favor of the belief, previously held by many observers, that certain types of minor illness, which accompany an epidemic of poliomyelitis, probably represent mild cases of the disease. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>